Done. Commit `ea82cca` sits on `session/page-transition-prototype`; `main` untouched at `bb85798`; the `.DONE` marker exists; build is clean.

## What was built

Every same-site navigation now runs through Astro's native View Transitions (`<ViewTransitions />`, zero new deps). The current `<main>` lifts off the stack with the site's own **ink-offset Card-Lift shadow** appearing as it leaves, and the next page settles in from below, losing the shadow as it lands — 320ms out / 420ms in, `cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1)`, gated behind `prefers-reduced-motion`.

**Files touched** (8 source + 1 new CSS): `src/styles/transitions.css` (new, all choreography), `Layout.astro` (router + GSAP→`astro:page-load` refactor + `ScrollTrigger` kill), all 6 `src/pages/*.astro` (`<main transition:name="page-card">`), `Nav.astro`/`Footer.astro` (`app-nav`/`app-footer`), `ChatWidget.astro` (`transition:persist`), `index.astro` (canvas boot/teardown), plus `docs/research/page-transition-prototype-notes.md`.

**Two decisions worth your eye,** both deviations from the brief, both explained in the notes doc:
- **Nav/Footer use `transition:name` + `animation: none`, NOT `transition:persist`.** The brief literally suggested persist, but Astro's swap code reuses the old DOM node on persist, which would **stale the `.active` nav-link** on navigation. Name-matching keeps them visually static while content still swaps underneath (active state verified correct in-browser).
- **New `transitions.css`** over `tokens.css`, so the whole prototype can be deleted wholesale.

**Verification — the brief's biggest worry (GSAP/canvas teardown) I actually tested.** I found a headless Chromium on this box and drove it via `playwright-core` (loaded ad hoc, `--no-save`, not in the diff): full 6-page nav walk, history back/forward, direct loads, reduced-motion emulation, mobile burger — **zero console/page errors**. Canvas `canvasBooted="true"` on every return home; `.reveal-word` resolves to identity (no stuck pre-state) after repeated nav + history-back; nav `active` resolves correctly.

**What I'm not confident about** (be honest): headless can't judge the *feel* of the lift, and a silver body-color gap may bleed between nav/footer as the full-bleed card moves — a real monitor + Performance panel review is the actual test. Also: the custom choreography only runs in Chromium/Safari 18+, falling back elsewhere; not checked for deep-scroll-start nav jumps. Details in the notes file.

`touch` of the `.DONE` marker is done. The two untracked files (`_transition_prompt.md`, the harness log) predated this branch and were intentionally left out of the commit.
